In order for the pod to be stuck pending, there would have to be 0 nodes with that hostPort available.

```
$ cat /tmp/deploy.yaml 
#!yaml
---
{
  apiVersion: "apps/v1",
  kind: "Deployment",
  metadata: {
    name: "hostport",
  },
  spec: {
    replicas: 3,
    selector: {
      matchLabels: {
        app: "hostnames",
      },
    },
    template: {
      metadata: {
        labels: {
          app: "hostnames",
        },
      },
      spec: {
        hostNetwork: true,
        containers: [{
          image: "k8s.gcr.io/serve_hostname:v1.4",
          imagePullPolicy: "IfNotPresent",
          name: "serve-hostname",
          ports: [{
            containerPort: 9376,
            hostPort: 9376
          }],
          resources: {
            requests: {
              cpu: "10m",
            },
          },
        }],
      },
    },
  },
}
```

Run that on my 3-node cluster:

```
$ k --context=diy get pods
NAME                         READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE
hostport-56c77d9558-6mtqc    1/1     Running   0          42s
hostport-56c77d9558-lg7zj    1/1     Running   0          42s
hostport-56c77d9558-x7rlc    1/1     Running   0          42s
```

Now remove the hostPort and apply, 1 goes pending:

```
$ k --context=diy get pods
NAME                         READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE
hostport-56c77d9558-6mtqc    1/1     Running   0          3m47s
hostport-56c77d9558-lg7zj    1/1     Running   0          3m47s
hostport-56c77d9558-x7rlc    1/1     Running   0          3m47s
hostport-6f49dbf957-llt77    0/1     Pending   0          2m44s
```

Why?  Because I asked it to create new pods before killing old ones, but there are no nodes where that is possible.

If I change the assumptions and have less replicas than nodes:

```
$ k --context=diy get pods
NAME                         READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE
hostport-56c77d9558-2sbls    1/1     Running   0          2s
hostport-56c77d9558-x9tdf    1/1     Running   0          3s
```

An update to remove hostPort starts 1 pod on node3, then kills 1.  Then starts a new 1, then kills 1.  After a few seconds it settles.

So I suspect this issue has more to do with not having nodes with ports available than with where explicit defaults get set.

If there's a real repro that works today, please reopen - I can't repro unless I force it into a corner.

